# Level 15 → 16 — SSL/TLS Encrypted Communication with OpenSSL

## Level Goal
Submit the current level's password to the service running on `localhost:30001` — which uses SSL/TLS encryption — to retrieve the next password.

## Description
Unlike the previous level, this service requires an encrypted connection. Plain tools like `nc` will fail because they send unencrypted data. This level introduces `openssl s_client` — a tool for manually interacting with SSL/TLS encrypted services.

## Approach
I first tried Netcat, which produced garbled output because the server expected a TLS handshake. The correct tool was:

```bash
openssl s_client -connect localhost:30001
```

After the encrypted connection was established, I pasted the current level's password and pressed Enter. The server responded with the next password.

## Command Breakdown

| Part | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `openssl` | Cryptographic operations toolkit |
| `s_client` | SSL/TLS client mode |
| `-connect` | Specify target host and port |
| `localhost:30001` | Target encrypted service |

## Why Other Methods Fail

| Tool | Reason |
|---|---|
| `nc localhost 30001` | Sends plain text — server expects TLS handshake |
| `telnet localhost 30001` | Unencrypted protocol — incompatible with TLS |

> Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]

## Key Takeaway
Not all services accept plain TCP communication. TLS is the encryption layer behind HTTPS, secure email, and most modern network services. `openssl s_client` is widely used in security testing to manually inspect and interact with encrypted services — for example, verifying certificates or testing for SSL misconfigurations.
